":[],"id":"unfold-two-faces","label":"Unfold adjacent faces","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unfold-two-faces":["cnm_051","cnm_054","cnm_185","cnm_183"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_051","cnm_054","cnm_185","cnm_183"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_139"],"id":"unfold-and-bound-all-paths","label":"Unfold two faces and prove minimality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"unfold-and-bound-all-paths":["cnm_051","cnm_054","cnm_185","cnm_183","cnm_190"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_051","cnm_054","cnm_185","cnm_183","cnm_190","cnm_055","cnm_139"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (10), with D=(7,3)","reason":"The midpoint sides are half the original diagonals, so AC and BD must be perpendicular and equally long. Since AC=(2,-6), BD must be (6,2) or (-6,-2). These give D=(7,3) or (-5,-1); only the first is in the first quadrant, and 7+3=10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"midsegments-and-diagonals","label":"Midsegments and diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midsegments-and-diagonals":["cnm_185","cnm_h168","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_h168","cnm_h173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (3 square inches)","reason":"Let T = AB ∩ XC. Triangle XYC has area 8/2 = 4. The midpoint relations give XT = XC/2 and AT = YC/2, so triangle XAT has area 4/4 = 1. The shaded area is 4 - 1 = 3 square inches.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_8_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055"],"id":"solution:triangle-area-subtraction:d7597a68bce8","label":"Subtract the small triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-area-subtraction":["cnm_p150","cnm_p148","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_p148","cnm_185","cnm_055"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p105"],"id":"solution:trapezoid-area:d7597a68bce8","label":"Measure the shaded trapezoid","methodDirectNodeIds":{"trapezoid-area":["cnm_p149","cnm_p148","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p149","cnm_p148","cnm_185","cnm_055","cnm_p105"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (75 square units)","reason":"The angle-bisector theorem gives BG:GC=50:10=5:1, so area(ABG)=100. Since DE is parallel to BC, triangles ADF and ABG have similarity ratio 1/2, giving area(ADF)=25. Therefore area(FDBG)=100-25=75.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"compare-trapezoid-areas","label":"Compare trapezoid areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-trapezoid-areas":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_185","cnm_272","cnm_p149"]},"nodeIds":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_185","cnm_272","cnm_p149"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 75","reason":"The angle-bisector theorem gives BG:GC = 50:10 = 5:1, so area(ABG) = 120(5/6) = 100. Since DE is a midsegment, triangle ADF is similar to triangle ABG with scale 1/2 and area 25. Therefore area(FDBG) = 100 - 25 = 75.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bisector-ratio-and-similarity","label":"Side ratio and similar-triangle areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bisector-ratio-and-similarity":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equal-distances-and-median-areas","label":"Equal distances and midpoint area halves","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-distances-and-median-areas":["cnm_128","cnm_185","cnm_269","cnm_113"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_128","cnm_185","cnm_269","cnm_113"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bisector-and-trapezoid-areas","label":"Use bisectors and trapezoid areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bisector-and-trapezoid-areas":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_185","cnm_272","cnm_p149"]},"nodeIds":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_185","cnm_272","cnm_p149"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 200","reason":"The square formed by the four side midpoints has both diagonals of length 30. Scaling it by 2/3 about P gives the centroid quadrilateral, whose perpendicular diagonals both have length 20. Its area is 20·20/2=200.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"midpoint-square-homothety","label":"Homothety of the midpoint square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-square-homothety":["cnm_185","cnm_275"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_275"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 15√3/32","reason":"XYZ is equilateral with side 3/2, so its area is 9√3/16. Each corner cut off by ACE is a right triangle with legs 1/4 and √3/4, hence area √3/32. The intersection therefore has area 9√3/16−3√3/32=15√3/32.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"subtract-three-corner-triangles","label":"Subtract congruent corner triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-three-corner-triangles":["cnm_185","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"subtract-three-corners","label":"Subtract three corner triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-three-corners":["cnm_p150","cnm_185","cnm_140","cnm_266"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_185","cnm_140","cnm_266"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (2√2 square centimeters)","reason":"Take A=(0,0,0), B=(3,0,0), D=(0,3,0), C=(3,3,0), and E=(0,0,6). Then P=(2,0,2), Q=(0,2,2), and R=(1,1,4). The midpoint M of PQ is (1,1,2), so RM=2 is perpendicular to PQ, whose length is 2√2. The area is (1/2)(2√2)(2)=2√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parallel-square-section","label":"A parallel square section","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-square-section":["cnm_269","cnm_185","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_269","cnm_185","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 40","reason":"In the listed cyclic order, the midpoint parallelogram has horizontal base 5 and height 4, so its area is 20. Joining side midpoints halves a quadrilateral's area, giving rectangle area 2(20)=40.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_8_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"midpoint-area-ratio","label":"Midpoint area ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-area-ratio":["cnm_185","cnm_272","cnm_p147"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_272","cnm_p147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"25/4","reason":"Let CE = x, so BE = 8 - x. Because E is on the crease, AE = CE = x. Thus 6² + (8 - x)² = x², giving 100 - 16x = 0 and x = 25/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_192_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"midpoint-similarity","label":"Use midsegments and similar right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-similarity":["cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 24n","reason":"MP is the image of AB under a half-scale homothety centered at C, so Q is the midpoint of CN. Since ON=CN/3, OQ=ON/2. Also [CMN]=[ABC]/4 and [OMN]=[CMN]/3, giving [OMQ]=[ABC]/24.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1966_AMC_12_Q38","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-and-midsegment-areas","label":"Use centroid and midsegment area ratios","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-and-midsegment-areas":["cnm_185","cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_272"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p148"],"id":"median-midsegment-area-ratios","label":"Use median, midsegment, and common-height ratios","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-midsegment-area-ratios":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, equality holds if and only if MNPQ is a rectangle","reason":"Triangulating along the two diagonals gives A≤(ab+cd)/2 and A≤(ad+bc)/2. Averaging yields A≤(a+c)(b+d)/4. Equality requires equality in both bounds, forcing all four angles to be right angles; every rectangle attains equality.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"midpoint-parallelogram-area-bound","label":"Use the midpoint parallelogram","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-parallelogram-area-bound":["cnm_185","cnm_183","cnm_111"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_183","cnm_111"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (nearest to 6)","reason":"The original triangle has area 6·6/2 = 18. The first shaded triangle has area 18/4 = 9/2, and each subsequent shaded triangle has one quarter of the preceding area. The total is (9/2)(1 - 4^(-100))/(1 - 1/4) = 6(1 - 4^(-100)), which is nearest to 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_8_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p179"],"id":"three_steps_and_remainder","label":"Bound what can be shaded later","methodDirectNodeIds":{"three_steps_and_remainder":["cnm_185","cnm_p148","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_p148","cnm_p150","cnm_055","cnm_p179"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3√5/4","reason":"The midpoints are E=(1/2,0,3/2), F=(1/2,0,0), G=(0,1,0), and H=(0,1,3/2). Opposite sides are parallel, and EF is perpendicular to FG. Their lengths are EF=3/2 and FG=√((1/2)²+1²)=√5/2, so the rectangle's area is 3√5/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"id":"triangle-midsegments","label":"Midsegments and spatial perpendicularity","nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_h120","cnm_174"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h151","cnm_055","cnm_174","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"],"id":"solution:triangle-midsegments:1424da505c3b","label":"Use triangle midsegments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-midsegments":["cnm_185","cnm_h120","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_h120","cnm_184","cnm_188","cnm_p089","cnm_h151","cnm_055","cnm_174","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 6","reason":"If the hexagon side is s, the triangle side is 2s because 6s = 3(2s). Each of the hexagon's six equilateral triangles therefore has area 4 × (1/2)² = 1, so the hexagon's area is 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_8_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_266","cnm_139","cnm_122"],"id":"matching-triangle-pieces","label":"Divide both shapes into matching triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"matching-triangle-pieces":["cnm_p150","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_185","cnm_266","cnm_139","cnm_122"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 12 + 12√2","reason":"Let AC = a and BC = b. The perpendicular bisectors of XY and WZ are those of AB and AC, so the common circle's center is the midpoint of AB. Equating its squared radii gives 5(a^2 + b^2) = a^2 + (2a + b)^2, hence a = b. Since AB = 12, both legs equal 6√2 and the perimeter is 12 + 12√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"id":"common-circle-center","label":"Perpendicular-bisector geometry","nodeIds":["cnm_133","cnm_187","cnm_185"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p188","cnm_110"],"id":"solution:common-circle-center:283b34f1a91a","label":"Locate the common circle's center","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-circle-center":["cnm_191","cnm_174","cnm_133","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_174","cnm_133","cnm_185","cnm_p188","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 12 + 12√2","reason":"Write a = AC and b = BC, so a² + b² = 144. The perpendicular bisectors of XY and WZ meet at the midpoint of AB, which is therefore the circle center. Its radius satisfies R² = 12² + 6² = 180 and R² = a²/4 + (a + b/2)². Consequently a² + ab = 144 = a² + b², so a = b = 6√2. The perimeter is 12 + 12√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"id":"perpendicular-bisectors-and-chords","label":"Locate the circle center geometrically","nodeIds":["cnm_133","cnm_185","cnm_251"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p188","cnm_055"],"id":"solution:perpendicular-bisectors-and-chords:35cc34c09191","label":"Locate the circle's center","methodDirectNodeIds":{"perpendicular-bisectors-and-chords":["cnm_191","cnm_133","cnm_185","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_133","cnm_185","cnm_174","cnm_p188","cnm_055"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 96","reason":"Let P be the medians' intersection, the centroid. Then PM=PC=(2/3)*12=8. Because the medians are perpendicular, area(MPC)=8*8/2=32. This is one third of area(AMC), so the requested area is 96.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10A_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"midsegment-area-decomposition","label":"Midsegment and area decomposition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midsegment-area-decomposition":["cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_p150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, (2+√2)/2","reason":"The smaller triangle has half the original area, hence side ratio 1/√2 and base √2. The trapezoid median is the average of its bases: (2+√2)/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1955_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_055","cnm_074"],"id":"similarity-and-midsegments","label":"Use similarity and midsegments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similarity-and-midsegments":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_185","cnm_069","cnm_055","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 8r^2","reason":"Take A=(0,0), B=(w,0), D=(0,2r). The circle has center (r,r), and the diagonal midpoint is (w/2,r). Its membership on the circle gives (w/2-r)^2=r^2. Nondegeneracy excludes w=0, so w=4r and the area is 8r^2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_260","cnm_p188","cnm_p089"],"id":"midsegment-diameter","label":"Find a diameter through the diagonal midpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midsegment-diameter":["cnm_262","cnm_186","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_186","cnm_185","cnm_260","cnm_p188","cnm_p089"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 3","reason":"If the triangle side is a and the hexagon side is b, then 3a=6b, so b=a/2. Each of the hexagon's six equilateral components has area 2(1/2)^2=1/2, giving total area 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_139","cnm_266","cnm_122"],"id":"equal-triangle-pieces","label":"Divide both shapes into equal pieces","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-triangle-pieces":["cnm_p150","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_185","cnm_055","cnm_139","cnm_266","cnm_122"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 5:19","reason":"Let H be the midpoint of DE. Since M is the midpoint of AE, MH is parallel to AD and DH=5/2. Thus HC=12-5/2=19/2. The parallels AD, MH, and BC cut transversals DC and PQ proportionally, giving PM:MQ=DH:HC=5:19.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_055","cnm_268","cnm_067"],"id":"midsegment-and-parallel-transversals","label":"Transfer the ratio using parallel lines","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midsegment-and-parallel-transversals":["cnm_185","cnm_269"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_269","cnm_191","cnm_055","cnm_268","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (1 quantity)","reason":"Let AB = b and let the fixed distance between AB and P's line be h. Then MN = b/2, the triangle area is bh/2, and the trapezoid area is (b + b/2)(h/2)/2 = 3bh/8. Only the perimeter PA + PB + AB varies.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2000_AMC_10_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_112"],"id":"fixed-base-and-height","label":"Track the fixed base and height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fixed-base-and-height":["cnm_193","cnm_185","cnm_p148","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_193","cnm_185","cnm_p148","cnm_p150","cnm_055","cnm_112"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 5","reason":"Write AB=a, DC=d, and EF=(a+d)/2. Equal heights give (a+EF)/(EF+d)=2, so a=EF+2d and therefore a=5d.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_034","cnm_040"],"id":"midsegment-and-trapezoid-areas","label":"Compare trapezoids of equal height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midsegment-and-trapezoid-areas":["cnm_185","cnm_p149","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_p149","cnm_042","cnm_055","cnm_034","cnm_040"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_034","cnm_040"],"id":"triangle-area-partition","label":"Partition the areas using medians","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-area-partition":["cnm_185","cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_p150","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_p150","cnm_042","cnm_055","cnm_034","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 12, with CI = (3 - sqrt(7))/2","reason":"Take A=(0,0), B=(3,0), C=(0,3), so M=(3/2,3/2). Write AE=e and AI=i. Perpendicularity of MI and ME gives e+i=3. The area equation (3/4)(e+i) - ei/2 = 2 gives ei=1/2. Since i>e, i=(3+sqrt(7))/2. Thus CI=(3-sqrt(7))/2 and a+b+c=3+7+2=12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"inscribed-angles-and-pythagoras","label":"Inscribed angles and Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"inscribed-angles-and-pythagoras":["cnm_253","cnm_137","cnm_185","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_253","cnm_137","cnm_185","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"12","reason":"The height from O to AB is 8/2=4, so the area of triangle OAB is 6·4/2=12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_186_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_112","cnm_064"],"id":"midsegment-height","label":"Find the triangle's height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midsegment-height":["cnm_186","cnm_185","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_186","cnm_185","cnm_p148","cnm_055","cnm_112","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1/6","reason":"The intersection's equatorial square has diagonals 1 and 1, so its area is 1/2. Each of the two pyramids has height 1/2. Their total volume is 2·(1/3)·(1/2)·(1/2)=1/6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p160","cnm_174","cnm_185","cnm_191"],"id":"two-square-pyramids","label":"Find the intersection and split it into pyramids","methodDirectNodeIds":{"two-square-pyramids":["cnm_h111","cnm_h107","cnm_189"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_h107","cnm_189","cnm_p160","cnm_174","cnm_185","cnm_191"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p160","cnm_174","cnm_185","cnm_p148","cnm_p163"],"id":"subtract-half-scale-tips","label":"Subtract the four tetrahedral tips","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-half-scale-tips":["cnm_h111","cnm_h107","math:similar-solid-volume-scaling"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_h107","math:similar-solid-vo